INT. LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

Fluorescent tubes, a row of humming dryers, a vending machine selling single-serve detergent. Rain on the window. The big clock reads 9:40. Orange plastic chairs bolted in a row.

AUGUSTINE BELLAMY, sixties, tall, sits stiff in a chair with a duffel bag between his boots. Beside him MARTA BELLAMY, fifties, folds the same towel for the third time. Their son LUCAS, twenty, slouches against a washer with his earbuds in. Their daughters, HANA, fourteen, and PIPPA, nine, share a dryer's warm glass front, sitting cross-legged on the floor. And in the doorway stands DESMOND, Augustine's brother, forty, rain-soaked, a suitcase in each hand.

Nobody speaks. Marta puts down the towel. Augustine looks at the duffel. Lucas pulls out one earbud. Hana and Pippa stop whispering. The dryers keep turning.

DESMOND
I knocked at the house.

AUGUSTINE
We moved.

DESMOND
I see that.

Desmond sets down one suitcase. Then the other. Water drips from his sleeves.

PIPPA
Is that Uncle Dez?

HANA
Shh.

Marta stands. Augustine stands. Lucas pushes off the washer. They form a half circle around the door, and no one reaches for anyone. Then Marta crosses to the vending machine, feeds it quarters, and returns with a tiny box of detergent.

She hands it to Desmond. He takes it, turns it over, and nods slowly. Augustine clears his throat and moves his duffel off the seat beside him.
